{"subjectId":"java","id":"design-patterns-behavioral","title":"Design Patterns: Strategy, Observer & More","group":"Design, Modern Java & Interview Prep","order":70,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Behavioural and structural patterns in modern Java — most of them are a lambda or a small interface away.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-route\"></i>Strategy</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Strategy</strong> — define a family of interchangeable algorithms behind one interface and choose one at run time. With functional interfaces, each strategy is just a lambda.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.Map;\nimport java.util.function.DoubleUnaryOperator;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static final Map&lt;String, DoubleUnaryOperator&gt; DISCOUNTS = Map.of(\n            \"none\", price -&gt; price,\n            \"festive\", price -&gt; price * 0.8,\n            \"flat50\", price -&gt; Math.max(0, price - 50));\n\n    static double checkout(double price, String code) {\n        return DISCOUNTS.getOrDefault(code, DISCOUNTS.get(\"none\")).applyAsDouble(price);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(checkout(200, \"festive\"));\n        System.out.println(checkout(200, \"flat50\"));\n        System.out.println(checkout(200, \"bogus\"));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>160.0\n150.0\n200.0</code></pre></figure>\n<p><code>Comparator</code> is the JDK's best-known strategy: <code>list.sort(Comparator.comparing(Person::age))</code> plugs a comparison algorithm into a sort.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-eye\"></i>Observer</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Observer</strong> — a subject keeps a list of listeners and notifies them of events; listeners don't know about each other. The basis of event buses, GUI listeners and reactive streams.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.function.Consumer;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static final class StockTicker {\n        private final List&lt;Consumer&lt;Double&gt;&gt; listeners = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();\n\n        Runnable subscribe(Consumer&lt;Double&gt; l) {\n            listeners.add(l);\n            return () -&gt; listeners.remove(l);          // handle to unsubscribe\n        }\n\n        void publish(double price) {\n            listeners.forEach(l -&gt; l.accept(price));\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        StockTicker ticker = new StockTicker();\n        ticker.subscribe(p -&gt; System.out.println(\"display: \" + p));\n        Runnable cancel = ticker.subscribe(p -&gt; {\n            if (p &gt; 100) System.out.println(\"alert: above 100\");\n        });\n\n        ticker.publish(99.5);\n        ticker.publish(101.0);\n        cancel.run();                                  // stop the alerts\n        ticker.publish(120.0);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>display: 99.5\ndisplay: 101.0\nalert: above 100\ndisplay: 120.0</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Forgotten subscriptions are a classic memory leak — always give listeners a way to unsubscribe. <code>java.util.Observable</code> is deprecated (Java 9); use your own listener list, <code>PropertyChangeSupport</code> or <code>java.util.concurrent.Flow</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-layers-subtract\"></i>Decorator</h3>\n<p>Wrap an object in another object with the <em>same interface</em> to add behaviour. <code>java.io</code> is built this way: each stream adds one feature (buffering, decoding, line reading).</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.io.BufferedReader;\nimport java.io.IOException;\nimport java.io.StringReader;\n\npublic class Main {\n    interface Coffee { double cost(); String describe(); }\n\n    record Espresso() implements Coffee {\n        public double cost() { return 2.0; }\n        public String describe() { return \"espresso\"; }\n    }\n\n    record WithMilk(Coffee inner) implements Coffee {\n        public double cost() { return inner.cost() + 0.5; }\n        public String describe() { return inner.describe() + \" + milk\"; }\n    }\n\n    record WithSyrup(Coffee inner) implements Coffee {\n        public double cost() { return inner.cost() + 0.75; }\n        public String describe() { return inner.describe() + \" + syrup\"; }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {\n        Coffee order = new WithSyrup(new WithMilk(new Espresso()));\n        System.out.println(order.describe() + \" = \" + order.cost());\n\n        // the JDK version: a Reader decorated with buffering and readLine()\n        try (BufferedReader r = new BufferedReader(new StringReader(\"line 1\\nline 2\"))) {\n            System.out.println(r.readLine());\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>espresso + milk + syrup = 3.25\nline 1</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-template\"></i>Template method</h3>\n<p>An abstract class fixes the <em>skeleton</em> of an algorithm in a <code>final</code> method and lets subclasses fill in the steps.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static abstract class Report {\n        final String render() {                      // the template — not overridable\n            return header() + \"\\n\" + body() + \"\\n-- end --\";\n        }\n        String header() { return \"REPORT\"; }         // hook with a default\n        abstract String body();                      // step subclasses must supply\n    }\n\n    static final class SalesReport extends Report {\n        @Override String body() { return \"sales: 42 units\"; }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(new SalesReport().render());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>REPORT\nsales: 42 units\n-- end --</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-plug-connected\"></i>Adapter</h3>\n<p>Convert one interface into another that a client expects. <code>Arrays.asList</code> adapts an array to <code>List</code>; <code>InputStreamReader</code> adapts a byte stream to a character <code>Reader</code>.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.function.Supplier;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static final class LegacyThermometer {                  // can't change this class\n        double readFahrenheit() { return 98.6; }\n    }\n\n    interface CelsiusSensor { double celsius(); }\n\n    static CelsiusSensor adapt(LegacyThermometer t) {     // the adapter\n        return () -&gt; (t.readFahrenheit() - 32) * 5 / 9;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        CelsiusSensor s = adapt(new LegacyThermometer());\n        Supplier&lt;String&gt; fmt = () -&gt; String.format(\"%.1f C\", s.celsius());\n        System.out.println(fmt.get());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>37.0 C</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-books\"></i>Where the JDK uses them</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Kind</th><th>In the JDK</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Strategy</td><td>Behavioural</td><td><code>Comparator</code>, <code>ThreadPoolExecutor</code>'s <code>RejectedExecutionHandler</code></td></tr><tr><td>Observer</td><td>Behavioural</td><td><code>PropertyChangeListener</code>, Swing listeners, <code>Flow.Subscriber</code></td></tr><tr><td>Iterator</td><td>Behavioural</td><td><code>Iterator</code> / <code>Iterable</code> behind every for-each loop</td></tr><tr><td>Template method</td><td>Behavioural</td><td><code>AbstractList</code> (<code>get</code> + <code>size</code> → everything else), <code>InputStream.read(byte[])</code></td></tr><tr><td>Command</td><td>Behavioural</td><td><code>Runnable</code>, <code>Callable</code> submitted to executors</td></tr><tr><td>Decorator</td><td>Structural</td><td><code>BufferedInputStream</code>, <code>Collections.unmodifiableList</code>, <code>synchronizedMap</code></td></tr><tr><td>Adapter</td><td>Structural</td><td><code>Arrays.asList</code>, <code>InputStreamReader</code>, <code>OutputStreamWriter</code></td></tr><tr><td>Proxy</td><td>Structural</td><td><code>java.lang.reflect.Proxy</code> (Spring AOP, mocks)</td></tr><tr><td>Factory / static factory</td><td>Creational</td><td><code>List.of</code>, <code>Calendar.getInstance</code>, <code>NumberFormat.getInstance</code></td></tr><tr><td>Builder</td><td>Creational</td><td><code>StringBuilder</code>, <code>HttpClient.newBuilder()</code></td></tr><tr><td>Singleton</td><td>Creational</td><td><code>Runtime.getRuntime()</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Strategy vs template method?</summary><div><p>Both vary part of an algorithm. Template method uses <em>inheritance</em> — subclasses override steps of a fixed skeleton. Strategy uses <em>composition</em> — the varying algorithm is an object (often a lambda) passed in, so it can change at run time and needs no subclass.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Decorator vs inheritance for adding behaviour?</summary><div><p>Inheritance fixes the combination at compile time and explodes into subclasses for every combination (MilkSyrupCoffee…). Decorators compose at run time: wrap in any order and any number, each class adding one responsibility.</p></div></details></section>"}